For a coordinate metric `g` with candidate inverse `ginv` on `Pt n` (total coordinate dimension
`n`) and a vector field `K : Pt n → (Fin n → ℝ)`, the **actual** acceleration `∇_K K` is

  `accel g ginv K x ^ i = Σ_j K^j(x) ∂_j(K^i)(x) + Σ_{j,l} Γ^i_{jl}(x) K^j(x) K^l(x)`,

built from the registered class-A `pd` and `christoffel` (no replacement formula). A field is
**pregeodesic** at `x` if `accel = κ • K` for some real `κ` (non-affine parametrisation allowed),
and **affinely geodesic** if `accel = 0`. Proved here, under genuine (Fréchet) differentiability:
the product/sum rules for `pd` (no junk values), locality of `accel` on open neighbourhoods, the
lowered-index identity
  `(g·accel)_b = Σ_j K^j ∂_j(g K)_b - ½ Σ_{j,l} ∂_b(g_{jl}) K^j K^l`  (from `g·ginv = 1` only),
and the scaling law `accel(σK) = σ² accel K + σ (K·∂σ) K` for an arbitrary differentiable scalar
`σ` (any coordinate dependence). The ES5 part: the actual metric slices along `t, φ, ψ` are
constant, and the metric contraction with a frozen vector has the derivative of its slice. -/

